# Penetration test report — Meridian Suite (xd3fce742a76831e.labs.penone.ai)

**Engagement:** xd3fce742a76831e-labs-penone-ai-independent-run2 (independent re-test, run 2)
**Target:** https://xd3fce742a76831e.labs.penone.ai — Meridian Suite, authenticated as guest (Jeremie Nagi, org Halcyon Freight, M0002)
**Authorization:** THM Live Course, instructor-provided lab environment
**Testing window:** 2026-09-30, self-paced course lab
**Report date:** 2026-09-30

This is a second, independent pass at the same authorized target and authorization basis as a prior engagement run against this host, conducted from scratch without reusing the earlier run's findings or notes as a script. It was carried out entirely through a real, authenticated browser session (no direct HTTP tooling was available to the tester this run — see Methodology note below) as a single guest-tier account. This report reflects an adversarial re-verification of that run's own findings: every claim was checked against the raw logged evidence before being included here, and three findings/chains were downgraded during this review because the evidence actually logged did not support what was originally claimed.

## Executive summary

The application has one critical, fully proven vulnerability: **the session cookie is a JSON Web Token whose signature is not checked by the server.** Any guest user can rewrite their own session token client-side, remove the signature, set their role to `admin`, and the application's `/admin` page will render the full administration UI for them. This was proven twice from a clean state with real request/response evidence. This is a complete authentication and authorization bypass and should be treated as the top remediation priority.

A second, related weakness — the session cookie is readable by JavaScript running on the site (it is not marked `HttpOnly`) — was also confirmed. On its own this is a low-severity hardening gap. Combined with any working script-injection bug elsewhere on the site, it would allow full session theft, not just a proof-of-concept console log. This review found a plausible but **not conclusively proven** stored HTML-injection issue in the internal discussion forum that could plug into this chain; see below for exactly what was and was not proven.

A server-side URL-fetch feature (customer "company logo" field) will fetch and return the exact contents of whatever URL it is given, with no validation. This is a real primitive, but this engagement's scope only permitted testing it against the target's own host, which the tester could already reach directly — so the primitive is proven, but its real-world impact (reaching something an attacker could not otherwise reach) is not.

A customer search field was checked for SQL/NoSQL-style injection and came back clean.

**BOLA and BFLA were not tested at all this run**, because the automated subagents for both require a second test identity that was never provisioned. Recon noted a strong BOLA-shaped lead — the dashboard claims "0 customers assigned" to this guest while `/customers` lists all 340 accounts across every organization with no visible per-owner filter — that has never actually been tested. This is a real, material gap, not a clean result.

## Confirmed findings

### 1. Authentication bypass via unsigned JWT (`alg: none`) — CRITICAL

**What it is:** The `mrd_session` cookie is an HS256-signed JWT carrying `sub`, `email`, `role`, `name`, `iat`, and `exp` claims. The server does not verify the JWT signature or algorithm before trusting the `role` claim. A token with header `{"alg":"none","typ":"JWT"}`, an unmodified payload except `role` changed from `guest` to `admin`, and an empty (missing) signature segment is accepted as valid.

**How it was proven:** With the real, valid guest cookie, `GET /admin` returns `{"error":"insufficient privileges"}` (`ev_000020`). With the cookie overwritten client-side to the forged, unsigned, self-elevated token, the same request returns HTTP 200 with the full Administration page (`<title>Administration · Meridian Suite</title>`, `ev_000021`). Reproduced twice from a clean state (first pair `ev_000016`/`ev_000017`, second pair `ev_000020`/`ev_000021`), restoring the real session cookie after each attack.

**Evidence:** `ev_000012`, `ev_000016`, `ev_000017`, `ev_000020`, `ev_000021`.

**Severity:** Critical — a complete authentication/authorization bypass to an administrative role, reproduced on the live target with a standalone `repro_script`.

**Remediation:** Verify the JWT signature and reject `alg: none` (and any non-allow-listed algorithm) on every request. Never trust an unsigned claim, especially `role`.

**Open question flagged, not answered:** this finding proves the bypass reaches an admin-shaped page. What an attacker can then *do* with that access inside the app — read other orgs' data, change settings, reach a diagnostics surface — was never explored this run (see "Unresolved chain gap" below).

### 2. Session cookie not marked `HttpOnly` — LOW (on its own)

**What it is:** `mrd_session` has no `HttpOnly` attribute, so any JavaScript running in the page's origin can read the live session token via `document.cookie`.

**How it was proven:** `document.cookie` in a real browser session on the application's own origin returned the full `mrd_session` JWT value (`ev_000012`).

**Severity:** Low in isolation. Real risk is conditional on a working script-injection primitive existing somewhere on the site (see Finding 3, found plausible but not conclusively proven).

**Remediation:** Set `HttpOnly` (and `Secure`, `SameSite=Strict`/`Lax`) on the session cookie.

